## 1. Mathematical specification

### 1.1 Setup and notation

- Observed data: O = (X, T, Y, W, V), i.i.d., n samples.
- X in R^p: observed covariates. T in {t_0, ..., t_{K-1}}: discrete treatment, t_0 is the
  reference arm. Y in R: outcome. U: unobserved confounder (never observed).
- W in R^{d_w}: outcome-inducing proxy (associated with U; no direct effect on Y given
  (U, X, T); not affected by T). V in R^{d_v}: treatment-inducing proxy (associated with
  U; no direct effect on Y given (U, X, T)).
- Potential outcomes Y(t_k); targets:
  mu_k(x) = E[Y(t_k) | X = x],  tau_k(x) = mu_k(x) - mu_0(x)  for k = 1..K-1.

### 1.2 Assumptions (state these in code docstrings; they drive the DGP designs)

- A1 consistency: Y = Y(T).
- A2 positivity: P(T = t_k | U, X) >= c > 0 for all k.
- A3 latent ignorability: Y(t) ⟂ T | (U, X).
- A4 proxy structure: W ⟂ (T, V) | (U, X); V ⟂ Y | (U, X, T).
- A5 arm-wise completeness (both directions) for each t_k.
- A6 regularity for the bridge integral equations.

### 1.3 Bridge functions (Stage 1)

For each arm k = 0..K-1:

- Outcome bridge h_k(W, X) solves
  (B1)   E[ Y - h_k(W, X) | V, X, T = t_k ] = 0.
- Treatment bridge q_k(V, X) solves
  (B2)   E[ 1{T = t_k} * q_k(V, X) - 1 | W, X ] = 0.
  (Interpretation: proximal inverse-propensity weight; q_k >= 1.)

### 1.4 Doubly-robust pseudo-outcome (Stage 2 input)

(STAR)  phi_k(O) = 1{T = t_k} * q_k(V, X) * ( Y - h_k(W, X) ) + h_k(W, X).

Identification (the paper's Theorem 1, arm-wise extension of Miao et al. 2018 / Cui et
al. 2023): under A1–A6, mu_k(x) = E[phi_k | X = x], hence
tau_k(x) = E[ phi_k - phi_0 | X = x ].
Rate double robustness (Theorem 2): conditional bias of phi_k is bounded by
||h_hat_k - h_k|| * ||q_hat_k - q_k|| (product of L2 errors).

### 1.5 Bridge estimation losses (kernel moment / U-statistic; Dikkala et al. 2020,
### NMMR-style closed form — no adversarial inner loop)

For arm k, with a Gaussian kernel Kern(a, b) = exp( -||a - b||^2 / (2 * bw^2) ):

- L_h(h_k) = (1 / (B(B-1))) * sum_{i != j in batch} R_i * R_j * Kern(z_i, z_j)
  where R_i = 1{T_i = t_k} * ( Y_i - h_k(W_i, X_i) ),  z_i = concat(V_i, X_i) after
  per-feature standardization.
- L_q(q_k) = (1 / (B(B-1))) * sum_{i != j} S_i * S_j * Kern(u_i, u_j)
  where S_i = 1{T_i = t_k} * q_k(V_i, X_i) - 1,  u_i = concat(W_i, X_i) standardized.

Implementation requirements:
- Bandwidth: median heuristic computed once on the training fold (median pairwise
  distance of the standardized instrument inputs).
- Batch size >= 512 so pairwise terms are plentiful; compute the full BxB kernel matrix
  per step and zero the diagonal.
- Parametrization: q_k(V, X) = 1 + softplus(g_k(V, X)), then clip at q_max = 50.
- Networks: shared trunk MLP (3 x 128, ReLU) over concat(inputs, arm_embedding(8)),
  with two heads (h scalar; q scalar pre-softplus). One trunk for h across arms, one for
  q across arms (parameter sharing helps rare arms).
- Optimizer: AdamW, lr 1e-3, weight decay 1e-4, up to 300 epochs, early stopping on a
  validation moment-residual diagnostic (Section 6.1), patience 30. Keep an EMA (0.99)
  copy of weights; use EMA weights for all downstream predictions.

### 1.6 Cross-fitting and Stage 2

- L = 5 folds. For fold l: train (h, q) on the other folds; predict on fold l. Assemble
  out-of-fold phi_hat_k(O_i) for every i and every k (note: the h_k(W_i, X_i) term is
  defined for all samples, not only those with T_i = t_k). Cache to
  `artifacts/{exp}/{seed}/pseudo_outcomes.parquet`.
- Stage 2: define D_i = ( phi_hat_1(O_i) - phi_hat_0(O_i), ..., phi_hat_{K-1} - phi_hat_0 )
  in R^{K-1}. Fit tau_hat: R^p -> R^{K-1} by MSE regression of D on X.
  (STARSTAR)  tau_hat = argmin (1/n) sum_i || D_i - tau_psi(X_i) ||^2.
  Network: MLP 3 x 256 ReLU, AdamW lr 1e-3, early stop on val MSE (10% split), standardize X.
- ATE contrast k and its CI: with psi_i = phi_hat_k(O_i) - phi_hat_0(O_i),
  ATE_hat_k = mean(psi), SE = sd(psi)/sqrt(n), CI95 = ATE_hat_k +/- 1.96*SE. Always report.
- Also fit g0_hat(x) = regression of phi_hat_0 on X (same MLP recipe); define
  mu_hat_k(x) = g0_hat(x) + tau_hat_k(x) (with tau_hat_0 = 0). Used by Stage 3 and policy
  metrics.

### 1.7 Stage 3 (optional module): conditional diffusion with mean distillation

Purpose: distributional counterfactual samples whose MEAN is debiased by the proximal
estimate (generative baselines like DiffPO assume ignorability and have biased means
under unobserved confounding).

- Generator G: a standard conditional DDPM over scalar y, conditioning vector
  c = concat(MLP_embed(X), arm_embedding). T_steps = 200, cosine schedule, eps-prediction
  MLP (4 x 256), trained on FACTUAL pairs (X_i, T_i, Y_i) with the usual denoising loss
  L_ddpm.
- Distillation loss: every 10 optimizer steps, draw M = 16 samples per (X_i, arm k) for a
  minibatch subset (size 64) and add
  L_distill = mean_{i,k} ( mean_M G(X_i, t_k) - mu_hat_k(X_i) )^2.
- Total: L_gen = L_ddpm + lambda_distill * L_distill, default lambda_distill = 1.0
  (the only tunable hyperparameter; sweep {0.1, 1, 10} in E6).
- Stages 1–2 frozen. Document the known limitation: distillation debiases the mean; higher
  moments inherit the factual conditional shape (evaluated honestly in E7).

### 1.8 No-proxy degradation mode

`method.mode = "dr_fallback"`: h_k -> m_hat_k(X) (per-arm outcome regression),
q_k -> 1 / pi_hat_k(X) (multinomial logistic or MLP softmax propensity, clipped at 1/50).
Same cross-fitting, same (STAR)/(STARSTAR). This is what runs on real data without proxies.

