## 4. Baselines

Implement in `opm/baselines/`, each behind a common `fit(X, T, Y, W, V) / predict_cate(X)`
interface. Multi-arm handling: one-vs-reference contrasts unless the package supports
multiclass natively.

Ignorability CATE (X only):
- S-, T-, X-, DR-learner and Causal Forest via `econml` (pin the version).
- R-learner: in-repo implementation (Robinson residualization with cross-fitted m, pi;
  document the multi-arm vectorized extension as heuristic).
- TARNet, DragonNet: in-repo PyTorch (~150 lines each; standard architectures).

Proximal:
- POR / PIPW / PDR with linear/polynomial sieves (in-repo, arm-wise; PDR uses the same
  (STAR) with sieve-estimated bridges — this doubles as an ablation of kernel vs sieve).
- NMMR (Kompa et al. 2022) and DFPV (Xu et al. 2021): adapt from their public GitHub
  repos if importable; otherwise reimplement the loss per paper. ATE-oriented; report
  ATE-only where CATE is unavailable.
- P-learner (Sverdrup & Cui 2023): pairwise one-vs-reference extension, documented as such.

Generative (E7 only): DiffPO from public code if runnable, else a conditional DDPM
identical to Stage 3 but WITHOUT distillation (label it "factual-DDPM"; this is the
minimal ignorability-generative comparator and is required even if DiffPO runs).

Baseline tuning protocol: fixed default grids (document them), selected on validation
factual loss only — never on test, never on causal ground truth.

