## 5. Experiments and acceptance criteria

Global protocol: 10 seeds (0–9) unless stated; report mean ± sd; statistics per Sec 6.3.

**E1 — Consistency and rates (DGP 3.1).**
n in {500, 1000, 2000, 4000, 8000, 16000}. Plot log-log |ATE_hat - 2| and bridge error
||h_hat - h_true||_{L2(test)} vs n.
ACCEPT: both curves monotone decreasing (Spearman < -0.9 across n); |ATE_hat - 2| < 0.10
at n = 8000 (mean over seeds); linear probe of h_hat on (W, X_1..X_5, T) recovers the
true coefficients (R^2 > 0.95 at n = 8000); CI95 empirical coverage of ATE in [0.85, 0.99].

**E2 — Product-bias (rate double robustness) check (DGP 3.1).**
Corrupt bridges with known rates: h_corr = h_true + n^{-alpha_h} * sin(X_1);
q_corr = q_hat_bestn * (1 + n^{-alpha_q} * cos(X_2)) for (alpha_h, alpha_q) in
{0.1, 0.25, 0.4}^2. Fit slope of log|ATE err| vs log n.
ACCEPT: fitted slope within ±0.15 of -(alpha_h + alpha_q) for at least 7 of 9 pairs;
corrupting ONE bridge only (other well-trained) still yields decreasing error.

**E3 — Main benchmark (S1, S2 + corruption scans 3.3).**
All baselines. Metrics: PEHE, ATE error (Sec 6.2), plus true policy value using known
potential outcomes. Corruption scans: OPM (non-oracle) vs proximal + top-3 ignorability
baselines.
ACCEPT: OPM beats every ignorability baseline on PEHE in S1 and S2 with paired Wilcoxon
p < 0.05 (BH-corrected); OPM >= sieve-PDR (kernel vs sieve gap reported either way);
degradation under corruption is graceful (no metric jump > 2x between adjacent levels).
If OPM does not beat ignorability baselines, STOP and debug bridges before proceeding
(check diagnostics of Sec 6.1 first).

**E4 — RHC real anchor (3.4).**
Pipeline validation via linear POR first (milestone gate), then OPM proximal vs
dr_fallback vs baselines. Report ATE + CI, q_hat diagnostics, ESS.
ACCEPT: linear-POR reproduction gate passes; OPM CI overlaps the linear-POR CI; all
weights bounded (max q_hat <= 50, En[1{T=k} q_hat_k] in [0.9, 1.1]).

**E5 — Semi-synthetic on real covariates (3.5).**
Same protocol as E3; this is the "realistic covariates + known truth" evidence.
Compare non-oracle OPM against baselines (NEVER headline an oracle variant).
ACCEPT: same statistical criteria as E3.

**E6 — Ablations (on S1 + E5 data).**
(a) kernel-moment vs polynomial-sieve bridges; (b) cross-fitting on/off;
(c) q-clipping q_max in {10, 50, 200}; (d) distillation lambda in {0.1, 1, 10};
(e) diffusion vs Gaussian-MLP generator (mean-matched); (f) dr_fallback vs proximal
under c_U in {0, 0.5, 1, 2} — at c_U = 0 the two must agree (sanity), and the proximal
gain must grow with c_U.
ACCEPT: each ablation table generated with the same statistics; (f) shows the expected
monotone pattern.

**E7 — Distributional counterfactuals (S1; truth from 3.2).**
Compare {OPM-distilled DDPM, factual-DDPM, DiffPO if runnable} on: per-arm mean bias
|mean_M G(x, t_k) - (g0 + tau_k)(x)| and 1-Wasserstein distance to
N(g0 + tau_k, 1 + ||beta_U||^2), averaged over a 512-point test grid.
ACCEPT: distilled mean bias < 50% of factual-DDPM mean bias on every arm k >= 1; report
W1 honestly including the variance mismatch (expected; it is a documented limitation).

**E8 — Diagnostics validity study.**
Across E3/E5 runs and corruption levels, correlate the Sec 6.1 diagnostics with realized
PEHE (Spearman, per scenario).
ACCEPT: proxy-strength rho_min and bridge residual each achieve |Spearman| > 0.4 with
PEHE across corruption levels in at least one scenario family; report all correlations.

